🚀 Kotlin/Native Gets Serious: LLVM 19 and Memory Improvements

The Kotlin/Native improvements in 2.2.0 are substantial, especially for production applications.

Per-Object Memory Allocation

// gradle.properties
kotlin.native.binary.pagedAllocator=false
// This enables per-object memory allocation
// Perfect for memory-constrained environments
class MemoryEfficientProcessor {
    fun processLargeDataset(data: List<ByteArray>) {
        // Memory is allocated per object, reducing overall footprint
        data.forEach { chunk ->
            processChunk(chunk)
            // Memory is freed immediately after each chunk
        }
    }
}

Latin-1 String Optimization

// gradle.properties
kotlin.native.binary.latin1Strings=true
// Strings with Latin-1 characters now use 50% less memory
class TextProcessor {
    fun processASCIIFiles(files: List<String>) {
        files.forEach { filename ->
            // If file contains only Latin-1 characters,
            // memory usage is automatically halved
            val content = readFile(filename)
            processContent(content)
        }
    }
}

Production Impact: In a recent Kotlin Multiplatform project with iOS targets, enabling these optimizations reduced memory usage by approximately 30% for text-heavy operations in the shared Kotlin/Native code.

🎯 Context-Sensitive @all Annotation Target

This small feature saves enormous amounts of time when working with annotations.

// Before: Tedious and error-prone
class User(
    @field:JsonProperty("user_name")
    @get:JsonProperty("user_name")
    @param:JsonProperty("user_name")
    val userName: String,
    @field:Email
    @get:Email
    @param:Email
    val email: String
)
// After: Simple and maintainable
class User(
    @all:JsonProperty("user_name")
    val userName: String,
    @all:Email
    val email: String
)

