1. Smarter overload resolution with suspend function

If you’ve ever had both a suspend and non suspend overload of the same function, you know how confusing it is, because compiler starts to show ambiguity error if you passed plain lambda. For eg.

fun log(block: () -> String) { println("non-suspend: ${block()}") }
fun log(block: suspend () -> String) { println("suspend: called") }
fun test() {
    log { "Hello" }
    // ❌ Error: ambiguous call between overloads
}

Only fix was to use ugly type casts to solve this.

log({ "Hello" } as () -> String)

But new version of kotlin resolves this clearly.

fun test() {
    log { "Hello" }           // ✅ non-suspend version
    log(suspend { "Hi" })     // ✅ suspend version
}

2. Concurrent Programming Enhancements:

Task API:

Kotlin 1.9.20 brings improvements to concurrent programming through the introduction of the Task API. This API provides a structured way to express asynchronous computations and handle errors.

val result: Task<String> = Task {
    // Perform asynchronous computation
    "Success"
}
result.onError { error ->
    println("Error: $error")
}.onSuccess { value ->
    println("Result: $value")
}

With the Task API, developers can elegantly express asynchronous operations and handle outcomes in a clean and structured manner, promoting better code organization.

2. Non-Local break and continue

Before Kotlin 2.1.0: Developers used labels to handle complex loops in lambdas, leading to verbose and less readable code.

fun processList(elements: List<Int>): Boolean {
    loop@ for (element in elements) {
        val variable = element.takeIf { it > 0 } ?: continue@loop // Using labeled return since continue wasn't available
        if (variable == 0) return true // Non-local return was already supported
    }
    return false
}

Starting from Kotlin 2.1.0: The new feature allows direct break and continue in lambdas passed to inline functions.

This feature expands the toolset you can use in the scope of inline functions and reduces boilerplate code in your project.

fun processList(elements: List<Int>): Boolean {
    elements.forEach { element ->
        val variable = element.takeIf { it > 0 } ?: continue  // Can now use continue instead of return@forEach
        if (variable == 0) break // Can now use break instead of return true
    }
    return false
}

To enable Non-local break and continue, add -Xnon-local-break-continue in the compilerOptions {} block of your Gradle build file:

// build.gradle.kts
kotlin {
    compilerOptions {
        freeCompilerArgs.add("-Xnon-local-break-continue")
    }
}
